  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 256 PM EDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nUpper ridging and surface high pressure will remain in control of \nthe eastern CONUS for the upcoming work week, and likely into the \nfirst part of the weekend. The upper ridge does briefly break down \nlater Wednesday into Thursday, with a weak and moisture starved cold \nfront stalling to our north across the southern Great Lakes before \nit gets into the region. The upper ridge does briefly build back in \nfor Friday, however it will be more progressive in nature. This will \nallow for some precipitation chances (20% Saturday) returning for \nthe second half of the weekend, more likely Sunday (70-80%) as \na shortwave ejects out of the central CONUS. \n\nUnder the persistently dry conditions, expect temperatures mostly in \nthe mid 70s early this week. Then by later this week, highs look to \nclimb more into the upper 70s with the aid of some southerly surface \nflow. Increased sky cover and precipitation through the weekend \nshould knock highs back down into the low and mid 70s, however these \nvalues are still above normal for this time of year. As has been the \nconsistent theme, look for some chilly morning with lows mostly in \nthe 40s through the work week. By the weekend, increase moisture \n(dew points) and increasing sky cover will allow for more mild lows \nin the 50s. We'll also likely continue to see some river valley fog \neach morning.\n\nWill continue to highlight the prolonged stretch of dry weather also \ncoming with a couple of concerns. 1.) Worsening drought conditions \nover the entire area, and 2.) Perhaps a few fire weather concerns \nlater this week as small fuels continue to cure and we get some \nslightly better boundary layer winds. At the very least, these \nconditions may just provide good conditions for some prescribed \nburns, but will have to make sure we don't run into a few more \nwildfire issues by late week. For now, good overnight recovery of RH \nvalues has been mitigating a faster small fuels drying out trend.\n\nThere remains a consistent signal for a more active stretch of \nweather going into early November, however individual details remain \nquite inconsistent with respect to the overall pattern. Timing, \nstrength, and placement of individual shortwaves passing through the \nmore progressive flow will remain in question for now.",
